            "text": "{\"primary_meaning\":\"A small, faithful minority of people who survive divine judgment and through whom God preserves His covenant promises, often representing true believers or the elect within a broader visible community.\",\"primary_basis\":[\"Isaiah 10:20-22\",\"Jeremiah 23:3\",\"Micah 2:12\",\"Romans 9:27\",\"Romans 11:4-5\"],\"alternatives\":[{\"meaning\":\"A specific future group of ethnic Jews who will repent and turn to Christ during the end times or the Great Tribulation, a view heavily emphasized in dispensationalism.\",\"basis\":[\"Zechariah 13:8-9\",\"Romans 11:25-27\",\"Revelation 7:4-8\"],\"prominence\":\"common\"},{\"meaning\":\"The literal historical survivors of the Babylonian exile who returned to Judah, viewed strictly in their immediate historical context rather than as an ongoing spiritual type.\",\"basis\":[\"Ezra 9:8\",\"Haggai 1:12\",\"Nehemiah 1:2\"],\"prominence\":\"common\"},{\"meaning\":\"A specific end-time denomination or movement (e.g., in Seventh-day Adventist theology) that faithfully keeps God's commandments and possesses the spirit of prophecy.\",\"basis\":[\"Revelation 12:17\",\"Revelation 14:12\"],\"prominence\":\"minority\"}],\"has_settled_consensus\":true,\"confidence\":\"high\",\"notes\":\"While the fundamental theological concept of a faithful surviving minority chosen by grace is universally agreed upon across mainstream traditions, there is significant debate over its eschatological application, particularly regarding the distinction between the Church and national Israel in dispensational versus covenant theology.\"}",
